%% ============================================================================
%% EML Example — Helpdesk / Support Ticketing
%% A simple, complete model: the data model (ERD), a declarative business rule
%% (ticket prioritization), and a workflow (ticket lifecycle state machine)
%% plus lifecycle hooks. Every diagram is valid, renderable Mermaid.
%%
%% Reporting: the questions written for each role by hand sit beside charted reports derived from the
%% model's own structure (its lifecycles, enumerated columns, dates, flags, measures, parents and optional
%% fields), and a %%dashboard per job groups them. Every role can run at least 50 charted reports and has
%% at least 10 dashboards of three or more.
%% ============================================================================

%% ---- Section 1: ERD --------------------------------------------------------
%%meta name: Helpdesk
%%meta kind: erd
%%meta version: 1.0.0
%%enum TicketStatus: open, in_progress, resolved, closed
%%enum TicketPriority: low, medium, high, urgent
%%enum CustomerTier: free, pro, enterprise
erDiagram
    Customer {
        string id PK
        string email UK
        string name
        string tier
        boolean is_active
    }

    Agent {
        string  id PK
        string  email UK
        string  name
        boolean is_active
    }

    Ticket {
        string  id PK
        string  customer_id FK
        string  agent_id FK OPTIONAL
        string  subject
        text    description OPTIONAL
        integer impact_score
        string  priority OPTIONAL
        string  status OPTIONAL
        datetime in_progress_at OPTIONAL
        datetime closed_at OPTIONAL
        datetime resolved_at OPTIONAL
    }

    Comment {
        string  id PK
        string  ticket_id FK
        string  author
        text    body
        boolean is_internal
    }

    Customer ||--o{ Ticket  : "opens"
    Agent    ||--o{ Ticket  : "assigned"
    Ticket   ||--o{ Comment : "has"

    %%field Customer.tier enum: CustomerTier
    %%field Ticket.status enum: TicketStatus
    %%field Ticket.priority enum: TicketPriority
    %%index Ticket(customer_id, status)
    %%index Customer(email) unique
    %%entity Ticket audited: true

    %%entity Customer help: An organisation or individual entitled to support, and the reason a ticket is answered at all. The tier on this record is what decides how quickly a ticket is picked up, so a customer is set up before their first ticket rather than created alongside it.
    %%field Customer.email help: The address support correspondence is sent to and the customer's identity in the system. Unique across the helpdesk — two records with the same address mean two histories for one customer, and the agent sees whichever they happen to open.
    %%field Customer.name help: The organisation as the agent should address it in a reply. Shown wherever a ticket names its customer, so use the trading name rather than the legal entity.
    %%field Customer.tier help: free — best effort, no target; pro — business-hours response; enterprise — round-the-clock, with an account manager. The tier is the support contract in one column and is set from the contract, never from the ticket.
    %%field Customer.is_active help: Whether the customer's support entitlement still stands. Cleared when a contract lapses; their ticket history stays readable, and new tickets should not be opened against them.

    %%entity Agent help: A member of the support team who answers tickets. Agents are the assignable side of a ticket: an unassigned ticket has no agent, and the lifecycle cannot leave `open` until one takes it.
    %%field Agent.email help: The agent's work address — how they sign in, and where assignment notifications go. Unique, because it identifies the person rather than describing them.
    %%field Agent.name help: The name that appears to the customer on a reply. Keep it the name the team actually uses in correspondence, not an internal handle.
    %%field Agent.is_active help: Whether the agent is still taking work. Cleared when someone leaves the team or goes on extended absence; their closed tickets stay attributed to them, but they should not appear in the assignment list.

    %%entity Ticket help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. A ticket is the unit of work the whole helpdesk is organised around: it carries the customer's account of the problem, who is answering it, how urgent it is, and where it has reached in its lifecycle. It is audited, because who changed a ticket and when is the first question asked whenever a response target is missed.
    %%field Ticket.customer_id help: The customer this problem belongs to. It decides which support contract applies and therefore how quickly the ticket has to be answered, so it is set when the ticket is opened and changed only if the ticket was raised against the wrong account.
    %%field Ticket.agent_id help: The agent who owns the answer. Empty while the ticket is unassigned, which is exactly the queue the team works from; assigning it is what moves the ticket to `in_progress`.
    %%field Ticket.subject help: One line saying what is wrong, as the customer put it. This is what the agent reads in the queue before opening anything, so it should be specific enough to triage from.
    %%field Ticket.description help: The customer's full account of the problem — what they did, what happened, and what they expected instead. Optional because a ticket can arrive as a subject line, but a ticket without it usually costs a round trip before any work starts.
    %%field Ticket.impact_score help: 0-100, how much of the customer's operation this is blocking. It is the only input to the prioritisation rule: 80 and above becomes urgent, 50 and above high, anything less low. Set it from the business impact the customer describes, not from how loudly they describe it.
    %%field Ticket.priority help: urgent — work starts now; high — today; low — when the queue allows. Written by the prioritisation rule from impact_score when the ticket is created, so an agent overriding it by hand is making a deliberate exception.
    %%field Ticket.status help: open — waiting for an agent; in_progress — being worked; resolved — an answer has been given and the customer has not disagreed yet; closed — finished. Only the transitions the lifecycle diagram draws are allowed, which is why a ticket cannot jump from open to resolved without being assigned.

    %%entity Comment help: One entry in the conversation on a ticket, from either side. Comments are the ticket's history in the participants' own words — the record of what was asked, tried and concluded — and an internal comment is how the team reasons about a problem without saying it to the customer.
    %%field Comment.ticket_id help: The ticket this entry belongs to. A comment has no meaning away from its ticket, which is why the whole conversation is read inside the ticket rather than from a list of comments.
    %%field Comment.author help: Who wrote it — an agent's name, or the customer's. Recorded as text rather than a reference because a comment can arrive from an address that belongs to neither an agent nor the account's registered contact.
    %%field Comment.body help: What was said. This is the substance of the ticket's history, so it is written to be read months later by somebody who was not there.
    %%field Comment.is_internal help: Whether this entry is for the team only. Internal comments are never shown to the customer, which is what makes them safe for diagnosis, escalation notes and anything not yet confirmed — set it before saving, because a comment cannot be unsent.

    %% Form layout: headed sections of two-column rows (`|` between rows,
    %% `,` within one; a row of one spans the full width).
    %%section entity: Customer; name: Customer; help: Who raises tickets, how to reach them, and the support tier their contract buys.; rows: name, email | tier, is_active
    %%section entity: Agent; name: Agent; help: Who answers tickets and whether they can still be assigned new ones.; rows: name, email | is_active
    %%section entity: Ticket; name: Ticket; help: What is wrong, in the customer's words, and whose problem it is.; rows: subject | customer_id, agent_id | description
    %%section entity: Ticket; name: Triage; help: How badly the customer is affected and so how soon it must be answered; rules set the priority from the impact.; rows: impact_score, priority
    %%section entity: Comment; name: Comment; help: One reply on the ticket, who wrote it, and whether the customer can see it.; rows: ticket_id, author | is_internal | body

%% ---- Section 2: Business rule — ticket prioritization ----------------------
%% Decision reads the ticket's own impact_score, so it evaluates on real data.
%%meta name: Ticket Prioritization
%%meta kind: rules
%%rule prioritize on Ticket event: beforeCreate priority: 10
flowchart TD
    A([Start: Ticket Created]) --> B{impact_score >= 80?}
    B -->|Yes| P1[Set Priority urgent]
    B -->|No| C{impact_score >= 50?}
    C -->|Yes| P2[Set Priority high]
    C -->|No| P3[Set Priority low]
    P1 --> E([End: Prioritized])
    P2 --> E
    P3 --> E

%% ---- Section 3: Workflow — ticket lifecycle (state machine) ----------------
%%meta name: Ticket Lifecycle
%%meta kind: workflow
%%workflow TicketLifecycle entity: Ticket kind: state
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> open
    open        --> in_progress : assign
    open        --> closed      : cancel
    in_progress --> resolved    : resolve
    resolved    --> in_progress : reopen
    resolved    --> closed      : close
    closed      --> [*]

    %%rbac role:agent|admin on Ticket.update
    %%report customer-by-tier title: Customers by tier entity: Customer chart: pie x: tier y: records help: An organisation or individual entitled to support, and the reason a ticket is answered at all. How customers divide across tier, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(tier::text, '(unset)') AS tier, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_customer WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report customer-created-per-month title: Customers created per month entity: Customer chart: line x: month y: records help: An organisation or individual entitled to support, and the reason a ticket is answered at all. New customers per month over two years - the volume the business actually handles. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_customer WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report customer-created-by-weekday title: Customers created by day of the week entity: Customer chart: bar x: weekday y: records help: An organisation or individual entitled to support, and the reason a ticket is answered at all. Which days customers are recorded on, which is the working rhythm of the people who enter them. sql: SELECT to_char(created_at, 'Dy') AS weekday, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_customer WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(isodow FROM created_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(isodow FROM created_at)
    %%report customer-changed-per-month title: Customers last changed per month entity: Customer chart: line x: month y: records help: An organisation or individual entitled to support, and the reason a ticket is answered at all. How recently customers were touched - a falling line is a register nobody is keeping up. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', updated_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_customer WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND updated_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report customer-age-bands title: How recently customers were changed entity: Customer chart: bar x: last_changed y: records help: An organisation or individual entitled to support, and the reason a ticket is answered at all. Records by time since their last change; the oldest band is the stale tail to review or retire. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '30 days' THEN 'last 30 days' WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '90 days' THEN '30 to 90 days' WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '180 days' THEN '90 to 180 days' ELSE 'older than 180 days' END AS last_changed, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_customer WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY MIN(updated_at) DESC
    %%report customer-edit-churn title: How often customers are edited entity: Customer chart: bar x: edits y: records help: An organisation or individual entitled to support, and the reason a ticket is answered at all. Records by how many times they have been saved; the heavily edited ones are where the process is being fought. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN version <= 1 THEN 'never edited' WHEN version <= 3 THEN 'edited once or twice' WHEN version <= 9 THEN 'edited 3 to 8 times' ELSE 'edited 9 or more times' END AS edits, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_customer WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY MIN(version)
    %%report customer-removed-per-month title: Customers removed per month entity: Customer chart: line x: month y: removed help: An organisation or individual entitled to support, and the reason a ticket is answered at all. Records withdrawn from use each month; a rising line means the register is being cleared faster than it is kept. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', deleted_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS removed FROM bus_customer WHERE deleted_at IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report customer-is-active-share title: Customers: is active entity: Customer chart: pie x: is_active y: records help: An organisation or individual entitled to support, and the reason a ticket is answered at all. The share of customers where is active holds against those where it does not. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN is_active THEN 'yes' ELSE 'no' END AS is_active, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_customer WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report customer-created-by-calendar-month title: Customers by time of year entity: Customer chart: bar x: calendar_month y: records help: An organisation or individual entitled to support, and the reason a ticket is answered at all. Customers created in each calendar month across all years, which shows seasonality the month-by-month line hides. sql: SELECT to_char(created_at, 'Mon') AS calendar_month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_customer WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(month FROM created_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(month FROM created_at)
    %%report customer-created-by-hour title: Customers created by hour of day entity: Customer chart: bar x: hour y: records help: An organisation or individual entitled to support, and the reason a ticket is answered at all. The hours of the day customers are recorded in, which is when the people who enter them are working. sql: SELECT EXTRACT(hour FROM created_at)::int AS hour, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_customer WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report agent-created-per-month title: Agents created per month entity: Agent chart: line x: month y: records help: A member of the support team who answers tickets. New agents per month over two years - the volume the business actually handles. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_agent WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report agent-created-by-weekday title: Agents created by day of the week entity: Agent chart: bar x: weekday y: records help: A member of the support team who answers tickets. Which days agents are recorded on, which is the working rhythm of the people who enter them. sql: SELECT to_char(created_at, 'Dy') AS weekday, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_agent WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(isodow FROM created_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(isodow FROM created_at)
    %%report agent-changed-per-month title: Agents last changed per month entity: Agent chart: line x: month y: records help: A member of the support team who answers tickets. How recently agents were touched - a falling line is a register nobody is keeping up. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', updated_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_agent WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND updated_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report agent-age-bands title: How recently agents were changed entity: Agent chart: bar x: last_changed y: records help: A member of the support team who answers tickets. Records by time since their last change; the oldest band is the stale tail to review or retire. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '30 days' THEN 'last 30 days' WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '90 days' THEN '30 to 90 days' WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '180 days' THEN '90 to 180 days' ELSE 'older than 180 days' END AS last_changed, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_agent WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY MIN(updated_at) DESC
    %%report agent-edit-churn title: How often agents are edited entity: Agent chart: bar x: edits y: records help: A member of the support team who answers tickets. Records by how many times they have been saved; the heavily edited ones are where the process is being fought. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN version <= 1 THEN 'never edited' WHEN version <= 3 THEN 'edited once or twice' WHEN version <= 9 THEN 'edited 3 to 8 times' ELSE 'edited 9 or more times' END AS edits, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_agent WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY MIN(version)
    %%report agent-removed-per-month title: Agents removed per month entity: Agent chart: line x: month y: removed help: A member of the support team who answers tickets. Records withdrawn from use each month; a rising line means the register is being cleared faster than it is kept. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', deleted_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS removed FROM bus_agent WHERE deleted_at IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report agent-is-active-share title: Agents: is active entity: Agent chart: pie x: is_active y: records help: A member of the support team who answers tickets. The share of agents where is active holds against those where it does not. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN is_active THEN 'yes' ELSE 'no' END AS is_active, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_agent WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report agent-created-by-calendar-month title: Agents by time of year entity: Agent chart: bar x: calendar_month y: records help: A member of the support team who answers tickets. Agents created in each calendar month across all years, which shows seasonality the month-by-month line hides. sql: SELECT to_char(created_at, 'Mon') AS calendar_month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_agent WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(month FROM created_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(month FROM created_at)
    %%report agent-created-by-hour title: Agents created by hour of day entity: Agent chart: bar x: hour y: records help: A member of the support team who answers tickets. The hours of the day agents are recorded in, which is when the people who enter them are working. sql: SELECT EXTRACT(hour FROM created_at)::int AS hour, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_agent WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report ticket-by-status title: Tickets by status entity: Ticket chart: pie x: status y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. How tickets divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report ticket-by-priority title: Tickets by priority entity: Ticket chart: pie x: priority y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. How tickets divide across priority, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(priority::text, '(unset)') AS priority, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report ticket-lifecycle title: Ticket lifecycle entity: Ticket chart: bar x: state y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Where tickets sit in their state machine, in the order the diagram draws it; a state nothing has reached shows as zero. sql: SELECT d.state, COALESCE(c.records, 0) AS records FROM (SELECT 'open' AS state, 0 AS position UNION ALL SELECT 'in_progress', 1 UNION ALL SELECT 'closed', 2 UNION ALL SELECT 'resolved', 3) d LEFT JOIN (SELECT status AS state, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1) c ON c.state = d.state ORDER BY d.position
    %%report ticket-created-per-month title: Tickets created per month entity: Ticket chart: line x: month y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. New tickets per month over two years - the volume the business actually handles. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report ticket-created-by-weekday title: Tickets created by day of the week entity: Ticket chart: bar x: weekday y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Which days tickets are recorded on, which is the working rhythm of the people who enter them. sql: SELECT to_char(created_at, 'Dy') AS weekday, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(isodow FROM created_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(isodow FROM created_at)
    %%report ticket-changed-per-month title: Tickets last changed per month entity: Ticket chart: line x: month y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. How recently tickets were touched - a falling line is a register nobody is keeping up. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', updated_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND updated_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report ticket-age-bands title: How recently tickets were changed entity: Ticket chart: bar x: last_changed y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Records by time since their last change; the oldest band is the stale tail to review or retire. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '30 days' THEN 'last 30 days' WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '90 days' THEN '30 to 90 days' WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '180 days' THEN '90 to 180 days' ELSE 'older than 180 days' END AS last_changed, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY MIN(updated_at) DESC
    %%report ticket-edit-churn title: How often tickets are edited entity: Ticket chart: bar x: edits y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Records by how many times they have been saved; the heavily edited ones are where the process is being fought. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN version <= 1 THEN 'never edited' WHEN version <= 3 THEN 'edited once or twice' WHEN version <= 9 THEN 'edited 3 to 8 times' ELSE 'edited 9 or more times' END AS edits, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY MIN(version)
    %%report ticket-removed-per-month title: Tickets removed per month entity: Ticket chart: line x: month y: removed help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Records withdrawn from use each month; a rising line means the register is being cleared faster than it is kept. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', deleted_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS removed FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report ticket-in-progress-at-per-month title: Tickets by in progress at entity: Ticket chart: line x: month y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Tickets grouped by the month of in progress at, looking two years back and one ahead. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', in_progress_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND in_progress_at IS NOT NULL AND in_progress_at >= now() - interval '24 months' AND in_progress_at < now() + interval '12 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report ticket-closed-at-per-month title: Tickets by closed at entity: Ticket chart: line x: month y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Tickets grouped by the month of closed at, looking two years back and one ahead. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', closed_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND closed_at IS NOT NULL AND closed_at >= now() - interval '24 months' AND closed_at < now() + interval '12 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report ticket-resolved-at-per-month title: Tickets by resolved at entity: Ticket chart: line x: month y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Tickets grouped by the month of resolved at, looking two years back and one ahead. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', resolved_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND resolved_at IS NOT NULL AND resolved_at >= now() - interval '24 months' AND resolved_at < now() + interval '12 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report ticket-impact-score-by-status title: Total impact score by status entity: Ticket chart: bar x: status y: total_impact_score help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Tickets added up on impact score, split by status, to show where the quantity sits. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, ROUND(SUM(impact_score)::numeric, 2) AS total_impact_score FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST
    %%report ticket-impact-score-per-month title: Average impact score per month entity: Ticket chart: line x: month y: average_impact_score help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. The average impact score of tickets created each month, to see whether the typical record is growing or shrinking. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, ROUND(AVG(impact_score)::numeric, 2) AS average_impact_score FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report ticket-largest-by-impact-score title: The ten largest tickets by impact score entity: Ticket chart: bar x: record y: impact_score help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. The records with the highest impact score; worth reading for outliers and for the few that carry most of the total. sql: SELECT subject::text AS record, impact_score FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND impact_score IS NOT NULL ORDER BY impact_score DESC LIMIT 10
    %%report ticket-status-by-priority title: Tickets: status and priority together entity: Ticket chart: bar x: combination y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. The most common pairings of status and priority among tickets, which shows which combinations the business actually sees. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') || ' / ' || COALESCE(priority::text, '(unset)') AS combination, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 15
    %%report ticket-created-by-calendar-month title: Tickets by time of year entity: Ticket chart: bar x: calendar_month y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Tickets created in each calendar month across all years, which shows seasonality the month-by-month line hides. sql: SELECT to_char(created_at, 'Mon') AS calendar_month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(month FROM created_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(month FROM created_at)
    %%report ticket-created-by-hour title: Tickets created by hour of day entity: Ticket chart: bar x: hour y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. The hours of the day tickets are recorded in, which is when the people who enter them are working. sql: SELECT EXTRACT(hour FROM created_at)::int AS hour, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report ticket-impact-score-quartiles title: Tickets by impact score quartile entity: Ticket chart: bar x: band y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Tickets split into four equal groups by impact score; the spread between the bands shows how uneven the values are. sql: SELECT 'quartile ' || q AS band, COUNT(*) AS records FROM (SELECT NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY impact_score) AS q FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND impact_score IS NOT NULL) x GROUP BY q ORDER BY q
    %%report ticket-in-progress-at-by-weekday title: Tickets by weekday of in progress at entity: Ticket chart: bar x: weekday y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Which day of the week in progress at falls on for tickets. sql: SELECT to_char(in_progress_at, 'Dy') AS weekday, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND in_progress_at IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(isodow FROM in_progress_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(isodow FROM in_progress_at)
    %%report ticket-closed-at-by-weekday title: Tickets by weekday of closed at entity: Ticket chart: bar x: weekday y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Which day of the week closed at falls on for tickets. sql: SELECT to_char(closed_at, 'Dy') AS weekday, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND closed_at IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(isodow FROM closed_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(isodow FROM closed_at)
    %%report ticket-per-customer title: Tickets per customer entity: Ticket chart: bar x: customer y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Which customer the most tickets belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS customer, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket x LEFT JOIN bus_customer p ON p.id = x.customer_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
    %%report ticket-impact-score-per-customer title: Total impact score per customer entity: Ticket chart: bar x: customer y: total_impact_score help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Tickets added up on impact score for each customer, ten largest first. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS customer, ROUND(SUM(x.impact_score)::numeric, 2) AS total_impact_score FROM bus_ticket x LEFT JOIN bus_customer p ON p.id = x.customer_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST LIMIT 10
    %%report ticket-per-agent title: Tickets per agent entity: Ticket chart: bar x: agent y: records help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Which agent the most tickets belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS agent, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_ticket x LEFT JOIN bus_agent p ON p.id = x.agent_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
    %%report ticket-impact-score-per-agent title: Total impact score per agent entity: Ticket chart: bar x: agent y: total_impact_score help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. Tickets added up on impact score for each agent, ten largest first. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS agent, ROUND(SUM(x.impact_score)::numeric, 2) AS total_impact_score FROM bus_ticket x LEFT JOIN bus_agent p ON p.id = x.agent_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST LIMIT 10
    %%report ticket-completeness title: How complete tickets are entity: Ticket chart: bar x: field y: missing help: One customer problem, from the moment it arrives to the moment it is closed. For each optional field, how many tickets leave it empty; the long bars are the fields nobody fills in. sql: SELECT 'agent' AS field, COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE agent_id IS NULL) AS missing FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'description', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE description IS NULL) FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'priority', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE priority IS NULL) FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'status', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE status IS NULL) FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'in progress at', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE in_progress_at IS NULL) FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'closed at', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE closed_at IS NULL) FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'resolved at', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE resolved_at IS NULL) FROM bus_ticket WHERE deleted_at IS NULL ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report comment-created-per-month title: Comments created per month entity: Comment chart: line x: month y: records help: One entry in the conversation on a ticket, from either side. New comments per month over two years - the volume the business actually handles. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_comment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report comment-created-by-weekday title: Comments created by day of the week entity: Comment chart: bar x: weekday y: records help: One entry in the conversation on a ticket, from either side. Which days comments are recorded on, which is the working rhythm of the people who enter them. sql: SELECT to_char(created_at, 'Dy') AS weekday, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_comment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(isodow FROM created_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(isodow FROM created_at)
    %%report comment-changed-per-month title: Comments last changed per month entity: Comment chart: line x: month y: records help: One entry in the conversation on a ticket, from either side. How recently comments were touched - a falling line is a register nobody is keeping up. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', updated_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_comment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND updated_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report comment-age-bands title: How recently comments were changed entity: Comment chart: bar x: last_changed y: records help: One entry in the conversation on a ticket, from either side. Records by time since their last change; the oldest band is the stale tail to review or retire. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '30 days' THEN 'last 30 days' WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '90 days' THEN '30 to 90 days' WHEN updated_at >= now() - interval '180 days' THEN '90 to 180 days' ELSE 'older than 180 days' END AS last_changed, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_comment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY MIN(updated_at) DESC
    %%report comment-edit-churn title: How often comments are edited entity: Comment chart: bar x: edits y: records help: One entry in the conversation on a ticket, from either side. Records by how many times they have been saved; the heavily edited ones are where the process is being fought. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN version <= 1 THEN 'never edited' WHEN version <= 3 THEN 'edited once or twice' WHEN version <= 9 THEN 'edited 3 to 8 times' ELSE 'edited 9 or more times' END AS edits, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_comment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY MIN(version)
    %%report comment-removed-per-month title: Comments removed per month entity: Comment chart: line x: month y: removed help: One entry in the conversation on a ticket, from either side. Records withdrawn from use each month; a rising line means the register is being cleared faster than it is kept. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', deleted_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS removed FROM bus_comment WHERE deleted_at IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report comment-is-internal-share title: Comments: is internal entity: Comment chart: pie x: is_internal y: records help: One entry in the conversation on a ticket, from either side. The share of comments where is internal holds against those where it does not. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN is_internal THEN 'yes' ELSE 'no' END AS is_internal, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_comment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report comment-created-by-calendar-month title: Comments by time of year entity: Comment chart: bar x: calendar_month y: records help: One entry in the conversation on a ticket, from either side. Comments created in each calendar month across all years, which shows seasonality the month-by-month line hides. sql: SELECT to_char(created_at, 'Mon') AS calendar_month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_comment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(month FROM created_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(month FROM created_at)
    %%report comment-created-by-hour title: Comments created by hour of day entity: Comment chart: bar x: hour y: records help: One entry in the conversation on a ticket, from either side. The hours of the day comments are recorded in, which is when the people who enter them are working. sql: SELECT EXTRACT(hour FROM created_at)::int AS hour, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_comment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report comment-per-ticket title: Comments per ticket entity: Comment chart: bar x: ticket y: records help: One entry in the conversation on a ticket, from either side. Which ticket the most comments belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.subject::text, '(none)') AS ticket, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_comment x LEFT JOIN bus_ticket p ON p.id = x.ticket_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
